course: dictionary
lesson: 14334
title: Formal words: obviate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Obviate is a formal word. It means to remove a need or a problem, so that something is no longer necessary. «The new online form obviates the need for a paper copy.» Nobody has to print a paper copy, because the form is online.
explain: Look at the pattern: obviate the need for something. The words after the need for tell you what is no longer needed. In the sentence above, a paper copy is not needed.
explain: In everyday speech, people say avoid, get rid of, or means we don't need. Swap one in: «The new online form means we don't need a paper copy.» If it still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Obviate is a formal verb, so you will mostly meet it in exams, reports and official writing.
fact: «Obviate» means to remove a need or problem, so that something is no longer necessary.
fact: It almost always comes in the pattern «obviate the need for».
fact: In everyday speech, people say «avoid», «get rid of» or «means we don't need».
fact: Its forms are obviate, obviates, obviated and obviating.
exercise: «The spare key obviates the need to wait in the rain.» What does obviate mean here? | Takes the key away forever | Starts a long wait | *Means we don't need to wait in the rain | Makes the rain stop
exercise: Which everyday words could replace obviate? | Explain it slowly | *Get rid of or avoid | Make it louder | Write it down
exercise: Finish the pattern: «A lift obviates the need for ___.» | blue paint | a red car | *climbing the stairs | the morning
test: «Clear road signs obviate the need for a map.» What is no longer needed? | Road signs | The road | A driver | *A map
test: What does obviate most often come with? | Sing | *the need for | a colour | yesterday
exam: Which sentence uses obviate correctly? | Obviate means a type of cake. | The lamp obviates is red. | I obviate tea every day. | *A bus pass obviates the need for a ticket.
